{"id":"14cc63d9-9f64-40f0-a37e-077000708109","updated_at":"2026-09-19T15:04:09.542Z","updated_by":"security@securecloudplus.co.uk","created_at":"2026-09-13T16:31:21.707Z","created_by":"security@securecloudplus.co.uk","name":"SMB (Windows File Sharing) Activity to the Internet","tags":["Tactic: Initial Access","Domain: Endpoint","Use Case: Threat Detection","Data Source: PAN-OS","Resources: Investigation Guide"],"interval":"5m","enabled":true,"revision":2,"description":"Detects SMB-related network traffic from an internal system to an external system, commonly using TCP port 139 or 445. This indicates network activity; it does not prove that files were shared, copied, or sent outside the organisation.","risk_score":73,"severity":"high","note":"Investigation\n\n1. Check the internal source, external destination, port, time, firewall action, and whether the connection was allowed.\n2. Confirm whether the destination is an approved file service, supplier, or business system.\n3. Check endpoint, Windows, authentication, and file-access records for the user, process, share, and any actual file transfer.\n4. Review related threat, unusual-volume, and repeated-connection alerts.\n\nResponse\n\nDo not automatically block or isolate the system from this network event alone. Confirm the business purpose with the system owner. Escalate unexplained or unauthorised SMB traffic, or confirmed file transfer, through the approved incident-response process.","license":"Elastic License v2","output_index":"","timestamp_override":"event.ingested","author":["Elastic"],"false_positives":[],"from":"now-9m","rule_id":"c82b2bd8-d701-420c-ba43-f11a155b681a","max_signals":100,"risk_score_mapping":[],"severity_mapping":[],"threat":[{"framework":"MITRE ATT&CK","tactic":{"id":"TA0001","name":"Initial Access","reference":"https://attack.mitre.org/tactics/TA0001/"},"technique":[{"id":"T1190","name":"Exploit Public-Facing Application","reference":"https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1190/"}]},{"fram